WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.620
Well hello there, I'm Tom Scott

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.480
as Herb mentioned

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.500
I'm from the University of York

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.250
although believe me

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.590
today I am most definitely

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.890
not representing them.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.150
In the next eight minutes

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.400
just like Hans Rosling

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.300
I have some graphs for you.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.650
To start off with, this is Twitter:

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.690
this is the times per day you post to Twitter

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.320
versus the amount I could give.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.430
As you can see

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.520
as soon as you pass one tweet a day

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.550
I couldn't give a rat's ass

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:22.520
(LAUGHTER)

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.400
and once you pass two a day

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.120
I couldn't give a monkey's.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.300
The pink line there, the little dotted pink line,

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.230
that's an exception.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.140
That's if I'm trying to get into your pants.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.530
(LAUGHTER)

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.290
This is the percentage of people

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:34.640
that understand the difference between

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.360
bar charts and line charts.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.700
(LAUGHTER)

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:40.540
It's kind of ironic

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.650
'cos that should really be a pie graph.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.680
(LAUGHTER)

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.440
This is the number of audiences

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:45.690
I've shown this chart to

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:46.530
That's the number of audiences

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.320
who've laughed at it.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.220
(SLIGHT LAUGHTER)

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:49.460
Hmm...

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:50.660
(LAUGHTER)

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.200
That's close enough.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.150
This is a pantomime.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:01:00.700
(LAUGHTER)

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.660
This is the time I intend to go to bed

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.470
versus the time I actually go to bed.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.470
The blue line is normal

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.930
The red line is if I have an internet connection.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.110
(LAUGHTER)

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.990
This is how awesome Hans Zimmer theme tunes are.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.500
Hans Zimmer is a very very famous film composer

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.590
He's been composing for years and years

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.340
First of all: Pirates of the Caribbean.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.610
(THEME FROM 'PIRATES OF THE CARIBBEAN')

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.350
Now, not only is that a fantastic bit of music

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:30.440
it's now become shorthand for pirates

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:31.920
You play that music

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.480
and everyone knows what you're taking about.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.700
That's quite awesome.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.290
Number two: Gladiator.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:40.400
(THEME FROM 'GLADIATOR')

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.190
Now, not only does that bring a tear

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.110
to the eye of grown men

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:43.560
and the end of the movie

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.950
That whole score redefined film music

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.640
ever since that came out.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.520
That's even more awesome.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.890
The last Hans Zimmer theme tune.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.120
(THEME FROM 'GOING FOR GOLD')

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.350
(LAUGHTER)

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:56.700
And you think I'm joking

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.110
you think I've just put that in for a laugh

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.110
but there it is!

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.110
Music by Hans Zimmer.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:03.180
Believe it or not.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.170
Can you imagine what would have happened

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.800
if Hans Zimmer had been in a different mood

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.330
when he'd composed the theme music

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.240
for "Going for Gold"?

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.900
(THEME FROM 'GLADIATOR')

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:14.930
The whole show

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.760
takes on this epic-battle-between-nations feel

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.150
(LAUGHTER)

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:19.700
and then suddenly

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.550
Henry Kelly walks on.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:22.870
That's brilliant, but

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:23.980
that's not quite as awesome as if

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.370
Hans Zimmer had been in a different mood

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.630
when he composed the theme music

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:27.790
for "Gladiator"?

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.100
(THEME FROM 'GOING FOR GOLD')

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.260
♫ 'Cos people are coming

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:30.900
♫ Everyone's trying

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.930
♫ Trying to be the best that they can

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.310
(LAUGHTER)

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.380
♫ We're all going for, going for gold

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:43.490
(ANNOUNCER) "Now, let's welcome your host, Henry Kelly!"

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.110
I think we can all agree

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.390
that that's significantly more awesome

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.810
than either of those.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.640
This is how much the DJ talks

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.400
versus how much I want them to die.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.120
The red line is me

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.420
the blue line is Morrissey.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.820
(LAUGHTER)

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.330
Two drums and a cymbal fall off a cliff.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:58.760
(INAUDIBLE FROM AUDIENCE)

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.000
Yes - b'doom, tssh. Exactly right.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.990
You see, you'd think that,

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:04.480
but we actually went and tested it.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:11.210
(LAUGHTER)

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:12.140
"Ready?"

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:13.110
That's Matt, a friend of mine.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.230
"Ready!"

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.340
"What's black and white

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.760
"and red all over?

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.970
"A badger in a blender."

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:19.760
Wait for it.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.450
(B'DOOM - TSSH!)

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:23.330
Yes!

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:31.690
(LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE)

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.500
This is how long I spend on an idea

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.310
versus the chance of it succeeding.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.210
Not that I'm bitter.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:38.330
(LAUGHTER)

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:39.350
I spent hours and hours putting together

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:40.420
a really complicated video about

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.370
Apple inventing a heads-up display:

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:42.860
nothing.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.420
I spent one, two hours

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:45.340
putting together a web site

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.780
that recovers deleted Twitter messages

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.220
from anyone's account:

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.520
goes everywhere.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.590
Fantastic.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:51.570
Gapminder!

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.150
Hans Rosling's Gapminder is an amazing thing

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.300
You can chart so many things on it

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.510
What I've done here,

00:03:58.510 --> 00:03:59.510
as you all know what's happening tomorrow,

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:01.230
this is the Eurovision Song Contest.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.430
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.600
As you can see, what we have here,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.640
is 1957, the date's at the bottom left.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.200
Those red countries, they're the Western countries,

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.730
they're the traditional Eurovision countries

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:10.980
and as time goes on

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.880
on the X-axis we've got the number of entries

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.250
the Y-axis is the five-year rolling average

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:18.410
of their score.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.700
Took me hours to put this together. Right.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.890
1994. Look at that!

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:24.210
See those on the left, the blue and the greens?

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.560
They're all the old Balkan states

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.120
and the Soviet states have all broken apart

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.340
and they've come into Eurovision in 1994.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.290
But look - on the right, all the red countries

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.430
they're still all over the place.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.240
There's still no pattern there other than

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.590
they've been there longer.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.210
But watch - the late nineties -

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.170
to the early 2000s -

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:42.650
televoting appears.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:43.700
Televoting appears in Eurovision

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.900
and watch those red dots on the right!

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.950
Down they go!

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.620
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.940
Not only do we have block voting,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.750
there's the United Kingdom, 2007,

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:55.150
look back here,

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.510
if I can grab that little thing there,

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:01.000
look at this, in the 1980s we're right up the top,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.130
that's Katrina and the Waves happening there,

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.900
the only one higher than us

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.150
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.910
is Ireland,

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.100
just about here, there they are,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.700
at that point the Irish broadcaster

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.670
was pretty much bankrupt, but watch this,

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:14.900
down we go

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.180
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.640
Televoting came in - and I can't think of

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:20.870
anything we could have done around 2002

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:21.890
that could have affected our standing

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.330
on the world stage as much as that.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.280
What else could possibly have that effect(?)

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.110
This is people who are red-green colourblind.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:34.230
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:35.150
As we can see from this graph,

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.520
everyone who is red-green colourblind

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.350
is a normal, productive member of society(!)

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:44.510
This is how long I've got left until I'm unemployed

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.320
It's not a joke, I just thought I'd mention it

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.570
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:48.500
This is Connolly's law

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.360
As time goes on

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.450
If you leave a man alone in a room

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:53.890
with a tea cosy

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:54.840
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.630
the probability that he will wear it on his head

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:57.900
approaches 1.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.480
I have some evidence of this -

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.710
This is Richard, this is a friend of mine

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:03.970
I left him in a room

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:05.410
with a tea cosy and a hidden camera

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:06.520
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:07.730
As you can see, at one minute

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:09.190
he has noticed the tea cozy

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:10.560
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.680
but at five minutes

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:12.610
he's still staring ahead

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:13.390
he's not going for it

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:14.600
then all of a sudden, seven minutes,

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:15.860
BAM! There it is!

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.480
(CHEERING)

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:18.780
(APPLAUSE)

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.130
And by eight minutes

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.290
he is raising the roof

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:22.310
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.450
and performing papal blessings.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:25.190
It's amazing.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:26.260
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.950
This, er --

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:30.000
(Sorry, what's that?)

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.000
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.540
This is the last one, the final one,

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.400
on the x-axis is the amount of effort

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.900
on the y-axis is the amount of awesome

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.510
if it's in the red zone, you do it

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:45.950
if it's in the blue zone, you don't do it

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:47.130
That's not a joke,

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.270
it's just a way to live your life.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.150
Thank you very much,

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:49.850
I've been Tom Scott,

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:51.700
good night!

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.700
(APPLAUSE)

